module gunbc.witness_floor_workflow

import extdeps.languages.yaml.types { yaml_string, yaml_int, yaml_bool, kv }
import extdeps.languages.yaml.emit { serialize_yaml }
import extdeps.languages.yaml.gha_workflow { project_workflow_to_yaml }
import extdeps.github.actions {
  Workflow, Job, Step, RunStep, UsesStep,
  WorkflowTrigger, Push, PullRequest, WorkflowDispatch,
  PullRequestActivity, Opened, Synchronize, Reopened, ReadyForReview,
  DispatchInput, InputString,
  WorkflowPermissions, PermRead,
  ConcurrencySpec, ConcurrencyMappingQueueNotMax, CancelInProgressExpression,
  RunnerSpec,
  checkout_action, setup_rust_action
}
import gunbc.ci_runner_target { gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec }
import gunbc.repo_identity { gunbc_default_branch_name }
import gunbc.merge_admission_produce { ci_repo_root_shell }
import gunbc.ci_layer_roots { witness_layer_roots }
import std.types { List, String, Int }

// THE ONLY CI EMISSION THIS REPOSITORY HAS.
//
// Authored fresh on 2026-08-15 as re-add #1 after the CI wipe, deliberately NOT by reviving
// gunbc.ci_workflow's job-spec architecture. That module emitted six jobs across a build ->
// regen -> ci -> heal -> fleet pipeline with per-step timeout rows, artifact transfer between
// jobs, concurrency expressions, upstream-red refusal arms and a floor step whose plan resolved
// batches, workers and a coordinator. None of it is re-derived here, because none of it follows
// from the obligation this workflow discharges.
//
// The obligation is exactly one sentence: every test witness in the repository executes, on
// every push and pull request, and the run is red if any of them is. From that, one job:
// check out the tree, install the toolchain, build the one binary that folds the witnesses, run
// it once. There is no second job, so there is nothing to sequence, nothing to hand an artifact
// to, and no `needs` edge to get wrong.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, so that a later reader does not restore it as an oversight:
// heal, the generated-artifact drift gates, the seven effect gates, the fmt gate,
// merge-admission stamping and the falsifier cadence are all real obligations and all currently
// unmet. They return one at a time, each re-derived from its own first principles under its own
// operator agreement. Their absence here is the declared state of the ladder, not a gap in this
// module.
//
// REGEN WAS ABSENT FOR A DIFFERENT REASON THAN THE REST OF THE QUEUE, AND IS NOW BACK
// (corrected 2026-08-19; supersedes the three paragraphs this replaced, which reasoned about a
// still-open integration/v1-cut race between two candidate cuts). Neither candidate the prior
// text weighed is what actually happened: integration/v1-cut never reached main, and the v1 .dag
// compiler authority was never deleted. Instead the REGEN ROOT CUT (#8406, 3b431f34a9e,
// 2026-08-18) deleted the old `regen_stage0` binary and its RegenVerifyGate/SelfHostStalenessGate
// CI gates, and rebuilt required-regen as one fold in the same binary this job already runs:
// `claim_executor --required-regen` and `--required-regen-fixed-point`, backed by
// src/v1/stage0/src/required_regen_host.rs. The fold still calls the live v1 .dag compiler to
// regenerate stage0's Rust sources and compares the result against the committed, frozen
// src/v1/stage0/src/*.rs files -- its subject was never deleted, only its old CI harness.
//
// So the gap this paragraph used to describe as order-dependent is now a plain, ordinary one:
// the fold exists and has never been invoked from CI. The two steps below close it by adding two
// more invocations of the one binary already built for the floor fold, in the same job, after it
// -- no second job, no artifact hand-off, no new `needs` edge, for the same reason the header
// above gives for not reviving gunbc.ci_workflow's six-job architecture.
//
// THE DRIFT GATES DID NOT RIDE ON THE OLD REGEN EITHER, and still don't: regen failing is a
// self-host fixed-point defect (the emitted stage0 sources diverge from what the compiler would
// emit today), which is a materially different fact from the generated-artifact drift gate (which
// diffs THIS repository's checked-in generated files against what THIS commit's .dag sources
// would emit for THEM). The two steps below add a third, unrelated failure surface to the job;
// they do not change what the drift gate covers.

data witness_floor_workflow_job_id: String = "witnesses"

// The workflow's own name, hoisted to a declaration because a second consumer now joins on
// it. gunbc.fleet_desired_admission refuses a workflow_run payload whose workflow_name is
// not the required floor's; spelling "witnesses" a second time there would fork the name
// (DESIGN §3) and the fork would fail open, not loud — a renamed workflow would leave the
// admission comparing against a name nothing produces, refusing every candidate forever.
data witness_floor_workflow_name: String = "witnesses"

// CANCELLATION IS A PER-EVENT FACT, AND THE GROUP CARRIES HALF OF IT. Operator ruling
// 2026-08-17: every push to main must finish; only pull-request runs may be superseded.
// The predecessor collapsed both axes into one group keyed on `github.ref` with an
// unconditional `true`, so consecutive pushes to main landed in the single group
// `witness-floor-refs/heads/main` and each new push killed the previous commit's run --
// main's floor coverage was decided by push arrival, which is exactly the empty-observation
// narrow section 5 forbids: a cancelled run reports no verdict, and no verdict was being
// read as nothing to report.
//
// Setting `cancel_in_progress` false for main is NOT sufficient on its own. GitHub cancels
// PENDING runs in a group regardless of that flag -- it only ever protects a run that has
// already started -- so a main run queued behind a busy fleet could still be superseded
// before it ever executed. The group must therefore be unique per main run, which
// `github.run_id` gives, and the flag must additionally be false there so the two
// mechanisms agree rather than one relying on the other.
//
// The cost is that N concurrent main runs may execute where one used to. That is the
// intended trade -- coverage of every landed commit, at fleet occupancy -- and it is the
// reason the dep-info race under `target/` is worth identifying before this is widened
// further.
data witness_floor_concurrency_group: String = "witness-floor-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.run_id }}"

// The binaries the fold actually needs, and only those. `claim_executor` is the fold's entry;
// `gunbc` is the CLI the repository's own tooling invokes. The predecessor build job named
// nineteen binaries, most of which this cut deleted; naming a binary that no step runs buys
// nothing and costs a compile.
// `v1_src_dag_parse` ADDED 2026-08-19 for the step below, which is the only thing that runs it.
data witness_floor_required_bins: List<String> = ["claim_executor", "gunbc", "v1_src_dag_parse"]

fn witness_floor_checkout_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Checkout" },
    id: none,
    uses: checkout_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [kv(key: "fetch-depth", value: yaml_int(n: 0))]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// CACHING IS DISABLED BECAUSE IT WAS DELETING OTHER JOBS' FILES MID-BUILD, and that is a
// statement about this fleet rather than about the action.
//
// setup-rust-toolchain defaults `cache` to true, which runs Swatinem/rust-cache, which
// registers a POST step. Two of that step's cleanup functions are the deleters, confirmed
// against its own source rather than inferred:
//
//   cleanBin(oldBins)    removes binaries from ~/.cargo/bin that were not in the baseline
//                        captured when THAT job started.
//   cleanRegistry(..)    removes directories from ~/.cargo/registry/src that do not match
//                        the CURRENT workspace's dependency set.
//
// Both are correct on a GitHub-hosted runner, where the home directory belongs to one job
// and dies with it. This fleet is self-hosted, and there /opt/actions-runner/<slot>/_work is
// per-slot while /home/ghrunner/.cargo is ONE directory shared by every slot on the host. So
// each job's baseline and each job's dependency set are computed as though it owned the home,
// and its post step then deletes against a home that other jobs are actively building in.
//
// The two observed symptoms are exactly the two functions. cleanBin gives
// `host tool unresolved: "cargo"` — cargo was present at 01:24:52 when the build step used it
// for 2m49s and absent later in the SAME job on the SAME PATH (run 32088114155), which no
// PATH misconfiguration can produce. cleanRegistry gives
// `ring-0.17.14/crypto/fipsmodule/ec/ecp_nistz.c: No such file or directory` during a build
// (run 32087371268, srv2-04), whose downstream noise is the
// `could not parse/generate dep info` class that reads like a target/ problem and is not.
//
// The selection effect corroborates it: the witness step is ~45 minutes and the build step ~3,
// so almost every hit lands in the witness step — which is what an overlap-probability
// argument predicts and what a configuration error would not.
//
// WHY DISABLING RATHER THAN SPLITTING THE HOME. A per-slot CARGO_HOME also works and was the
// first candidate, but it multiplies the registry by the slot count on every host to solve a
// problem caused by a cache layer this fleet does not need: ~/.cargo already persists across
// jobs on a self-hosted runner, so restore-and-save buys nothing here and only the cleanup
// half has an effect. Disabling is the smaller change and removes the mechanism outright.
//
// THE COST, STATED. Nothing now prunes ~/.cargo, so the registry grows without bound and
// becomes a disk-pressure question instead of a correctness one. That is the right trade —
// a full disk refuses loudly, a deleted source file mid-compile does not — but it is a real
// obligation and it belongs to whoever owns fleet disk, not to this file.
fn witness_floor_toolchain_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Install Rust toolchain" },
    id: none,
    uses: setup_rust_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [kv(key: "cache", value: yaml_bool(b: false))]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// One cargo invocation, no retry arm. The predecessor carried a fallback that re-ran the build
// with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 and RUSTC_WRAPPER unset when the first attempt failed; that arm made
// an sccache deficit unobservable by construction (DESIGN section 5, the absorbing fallback), so
// it is not re-derived. A build failure is a build failure and stops the line.
fn witness_floor_build_script() -> String {
  fold(
    witness_floor_required_bins,
    init: "cargo build --release -p v1-compiler",
    f: fn(acc, bin) { concat(acc, concat(" --bin ", bin)) }
  )
}

fn witness_floor_build_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Build the witness fold" },
    id: Present { value: "build_witness_fold" },
    run: concat(witness_floor_build_script(), "\n"),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// The source-root flags are FOLDED from gunbc.ci_layer_roots witness_layer_roots, never spelled
// here. That authority is what the compile-clean closure and the divergence census already read,
// so a literal list in this emitter would be a second representation of it (DESIGN §3) -- and the
// rung drop is invisible exactly while the bytes still agree, because the copy typechecks and
// looks finished. It went in as a literal on the first authoring of this file and is corrected
// here; the class was named by tidy-gull-813 on a sibling lane, same day, from its own migration.
fn witness_floor_source_root_flags() -> String {
  fold(witness_layer_roots, init: "", f: fn(acc, root) {
    concat(acc, concat(" --source-root \"$ROOT/", concat(root, "\"")))
  })
}

// ONE INVOCATION OF OUR OWN BINARY. Everything the run decides -- which witnesses exist, what
// identity each has, which frame it needs, and whether the roster is admissible at all -- is
// decided in .dag by v2.workflow.required_floor. This step passes the source roots and
// nothing else: there is no plan entry, no plan function, no batch id, no worker role and no
// selection flag, because the fold has no such concepts to configure.
fn witness_floor_run_script() -> String {
  concat(
    concat("ROOT=", concat(ci_repo_root_shell(), "\n")),
    concat(
      "\"$ROOT/target/release/claim_executor\" --required-floor",
      concat(witness_floor_source_root_flags(), "\n")
    )
  )
}

// src/v1's .dag SOURCES HAVE NO REQUIRED GATE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN
// OVERSIGHT. Two things were tried before this step, and both were measured wrong.
//
// (1) `--source-root src/v1` on the fold refuses. Measured, CI run 32219326621: one flat
// namespace over both trees yields 12 last-segment collisions -- `tokenize`, `parse`, `resolve`,
// `emit`, `infer`, `normalize` and six more -- because the v1 and v2 compilers name their stages
// identically. All 532 diagnostics that run produced were in src/v2 and dag/test; ZERO in src/v1.
// The blocker is bare cross-module reference binding, and it dissolves with namespace-only
// resolution rather than with a flag.
//
// (2) `test.claim.v1_dag_parse_witness.v1_dag_parse_witnesses` EXISTS and is discovered by the
// fold, which looks like the gate already being present. It is not, and the reason is the one
// `gunbc.ci_layer_roots` `excl_bin_wet_reason` states: this fold is HERMETIC, the witness reaches
// `shell.Test.IsExecutable` through `tools.host_prelude`, and the hermetic envelope refuses host
// effects by construction. Measured, CI run 32223611085:
//
//   FAIL test.claim.v1_dag_parse_witness.v1_dag_parse_witnesses errored:
//     type error: hermetic mode: no mock_response for operation IsExecutable
//     -- refusing to fabricate Unit
//
// That refusal is CORRECT and the witness cannot be made hermetic -- its subject is a subprocess
// exit status, which no in-process assertion can observe, and mocking it would let the witness
// pass against a fabricated status. So the row stays on `floor_expected_red`. What is actually
// missing is its executing consumer: `bin_witness_wet_entries` was the declared per-PR wet batch
// for this whole class, and it died with the old floor. The class has had no executor since.
//
// THIS STEP IS THAT EXECUTOR, for one member of the class, and it runs OUTSIDE the fold because
// outside the fold is the only place a host effect can happen. It is not a second authority over
// the witness: the witness cannot execute, so there is no verdict for this to duplicate.
//
// WHAT IT CLAIMS. Each src/v1 .dag file parses INDEPENDENTLY -- no shared pool, no cross-module
// resolution -- so (1)'s collision class never arises. One rung: a parse error cannot land
// silently. NOTHING about resolution, types, or whether those modules mean anything.
// `tests/fixtures/` is excluded; those are deliberately malformed inputs authored FOR the
// parser's own tests, and the existing corpus discovery in `compiler_tests` excludes them on the
// same grounds.
//
// THE BINARY IS A REGISTERED SCAFFOLD AND STAYS ONE. Its dissolve-on is the modeled pipeline
// parsing src/v1; wiring the scaffold does not satisfy that. Ordered BEFORE the fold because it
// costs 0.7 seconds against the fold's tens of minutes, and a stopped line is cheapest early.
fn witness_floor_v1_parse_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "src/v1 .dag sources parse" },
    id: none,
    run: concat(
      concat("ROOT=", concat(ci_repo_root_shell(), "\n")),
      "cd \"$ROOT\" && \"$ROOT/target/release/v1_src_dag_parse\"\n"
    ),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE FLOOR RUNS LAST AND THEREFORE DECLARES ITS PRECONDITION TOO (operator directive,
// 2026-08-20: "move regen before witnesses"). Regen costs ~4 minutes and the floor ~30, so
// ordering the cheap total check first turns a drift report from a 35-minute wait into a
// 5-minute one -- the ordinary cheap-refusal-first argument, and the reason the directive is
// right.
//
// But the reorder is not a pure move, and shipping it as one would have re-created the exact
// defect the two regen steps were added to fix, pointing the other way. A step with no `if`
// inherits GitHub's default `success()` -- the conjunction of every EARLIER step -- so once
// regen sits ahead of the floor, an unconditioned floor would silently acquire regen's verdict
// as a precondition. A regen red would then DISARM the whole witness floor, and 9,008 witnesses
// would report `skipped` because a stale stage0 mirror is a materially different fact that has
// nothing to say about whether the corpus's claims hold. That is the empty-observation narrow
// again -- `the floor did not fail` standing in for `the floor was never evaluated` -- and it
// would be strictly worse than the original, because the population silenced is three orders of
// magnitude larger than the one silenced before.
//
// So the floor's real precondition is stated for the same reason regen's is, and it is the same
// one: the binary exists. The three claims -- floor, regen, regen determinism -- are mutually
// independent except where regen's receipt genuinely feeds the fixed-point step, and after this
// change every step in the job declares what it actually needs rather than inheriting its
// position. Order is now free to change again without silently rewiring any step's condition,
// which is the property that made the original ordering a hazard rather than a preference.
//
// MEASURED CORRECTION, run 32323690924. The paragraph above was right about the hazard and wrong
// about the remedy, and CI refused the remedy rather than any reasoning catching it. On that run
// the build succeeded, so `steps.build_witness_fold.outcome == 'success'` evaluated TRUE -- and
// the floor reported `skipped` anyway, behind a regen failure. The reason is that declaring an
// `if` does not, on its own, displace the implicit `success()`: GitHub substitutes the author's
// expression for the default ONLY when that expression contains a status function
// (`success()`, `failure()`, `always()`, `cancelled()`). A bare `steps.*.outcome` comparison is
// not one, so the default is silently CONJOINED rather than replaced, and every condition below
// still read `success() && <declared>`. The declaration was decorative: the floor remained
// wired to regen's verdict, and the 9,008-witness silencing described above happened on the
// first run that exercised it.
//
// So each condition carries `!cancelled()` -- a status function, which is what actually performs
// the substitution -- rather than `always()`, because a cancelled run should stop, and only a
// FAILED earlier step must not propagate. The discriminator worth keeping: a precondition that
// reads correctly is not evidence that it binds. The tell here was a step skipping while its
// own stated condition was true, which is visible only by reading the step's outcome beside the
// condition's operands, never from the workflow text.
fn witness_step_status_guard() -> String {
  "!cancelled() && "
}

fn witness_floor_precondition() -> String {
  concat(witness_step_status_guard(), "steps.build_witness_fold.outcome == 'success'")
}

fn witness_floor_run_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "All witnesses (one prepared subject, one fold)" },
    id: none,
    run: witness_floor_run_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_floor_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// TWO MORE INVOCATIONS OF THE SAME BINARY, mirroring witness_floor_run_step() exactly: the
// `--source-root` flags are the same fold over gunbc.ci_layer_roots witness_layer_roots (DESIGN
// §3 -- one authority, not a second literal roster), and each mode decides its own subject in
// .dag; this step passes only the source roots. `--required-regen` proves the fold's first
// generation matches the committed candidate -- a genuine committed-vs-emitted staleness probe.
// Both currently require at least one --source-root flag to pass claim_executor's own CLI gate
// even though neither mode's function consumes it (src/v1/stage0/src/bin/claim_executor.rs, the
// empty-source-roots check runs ahead of all three mode dispatches) -- fixing that gate is out
// of scope here; supplying the flag is the correct interim, not a workaround, since the flag is
// otherwise meaningful for every other mode this binary serves.
//
// WHY THESE TWO STEPS WERE STRIPPED ON 2026-08-20 AND RESTORED THE SAME DAY, recorded because the
// strip's argument was CORRECT AT THE TIME and a reader who finds only the restoration will
// re-derive the strip. The admission test that removed them (deep-ant-102): is every red closable,
// by the author who caused it, at the moment they caused it? It was not. `--required-regen`
// reported 16 drifting files on a tree carrying every fix then available, and no sequence of
// regenerations could clear them, so the only accessible green was a hand-edited mirror -- a
// required gate whose only path to green is the violation it guards against, which launders the
// workaround under a green check and is worse than no gate. What changed is not the test, it is
// the answer: TWO INDEPENDENT BLOCKERS WERE ROOT-CAUSED AND CLOSED, and the gate now has a
// reachable green that is reached by regenerating, never by editing.
//
// BLOCKER 1 -- THE TWO-GENERATION LAG (closed by #8637 plus a staged install, no hand edit). The
// duplicate `SeedRetainedIntrinsicRegistration` row was deleted from the .dag authority, but the
// first regenerated `lib.rs` STILL emitted the bare `pub mod expected_red_roster_join;` with no
// matching file (E0583), because `lib.rs`'s module list is emitted from the COMPILED-IN constant
// in `gunbc_stage0_crate_layout_generated.rs`, not from the .dag read at runtime. The route that
// closes it installs the regenerated constant ALONE, rebuilds, and re-runs: the emitted `lib.rs`
// is then clean and drift drops 16 -> 15. Every step is an install of generated bytes; nothing is
// authored by hand. Recorded because the tempting alternative -- deleting the offending line from
// the mirror to make the build proceed -- is the author-side absorbing fallback (DESIGN §5), and
// it had already conditioned three "generation 1 builds clean" reports from this session that had
// to be retracted.
//
// BLOCKER 2 -- THE COMPARATOR HAD NO REACHABLE GREEN, and this one is invisible to every
// candidate-side check because the candidate on disk is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the committed file the
// gate is refusing. After the staged install, drift stuck at exactly one file,
// `v1_compiler_infer.rs`, at generations 2, 3 and 4 -- deterministic, with `diff` returning zero
// lines each time. `compare_generated_surfaces` normalized BOTH sides through rustfmt while
// `write_emitted_tree` writes `normalize(emitted)`; once a candidate is installed the comparison
// is therefore `normalize(normalize(emitted))` vs `normalize(emitted)`, an identity only if
// rustfmt is idempotent -- and it is not: on that file a `let x = if (long.receiver.chain)`
// re-splits on a second pass. So the check refused a tree that already equalled its own artifact,
// forever, and the only way to silence it was to hand-edit the mirror.
//
// THE FIRST FIX WAS WRONG AND THE PRE-COMMIT HOOK CAUGHT IT, which is worth recording because it
// located the real defect. Comparing the committed side RAW against the single-pass bytes made
// `--required-regen` green and `cargo fmt --all --check` RED on the same file: the fmt gate
// re-formats what is committed, so it demands pass N+1, while the comparator had just demanded
// pass N. The two gates were in direct contradiction, and satisfying either broke the other in a
// loop with no exit. So the defect is not in either gate's comparison -- it is that the emitted
// artifact was written in a form that is not a FIXED POINT of the formatter, and a non-fixed-point
// artifact cannot satisfy two consumers that consume different passes of it.
//
// THE FIX: `normalize_generated_source` now iterates rustfmt to a fixed point (bounded at 8
// passes, exceeding it is a typed refusal -- a widened arm there would be the absorbing fallback
// DESIGN §5 forbids and would restore the unclosable state), and the committed side is compared
// RAW against exactly those bytes. `cargo fmt` is then a no-op on the artifact by definition, so
// the contradiction is unrepresentable rather than detected: construction over validation
// (DESIGN §5), and the compared artifact and the written artifact become ONE derivation instead
// of two (DESIGN §3), which is what makes "install the candidate" a guaranteed remedy.
// Verified in both directions on the installed candidate: `cargo fmt --all --check` exits 0 AND
// `--required-regen` exits 0.
//
// EVIDENCE FOR THE RESTORATION, executed rather than argued: `--required-regen` exits 0 with
// `first_generation_equal=true planned=129 executed=129`; `--required-regen-fixed-point` exits 0
// with `fixed_point_equal=true`; and the discriminating RED refuses -- one comment line prepended
// to a generated file yields exit 1 naming `std_pareto.rs`, with the restore verified afterwards.
// A green without that RED would establish nothing, since this comparator's failure mode was
// precisely a verdict that could not respond to the tree.
//
// COST, measured over seven full passes on an arm64 session container: `elapsed_ms` 206705,
// 220041, 221086, 231461, 234069, 235851, 253731 -- 3.4 to 4.2 minutes per pass. The fixed-point
// step performs a second full emit, so the pair adds roughly 8 minutes to a ~30-minute job. That
// is the price of the gate and it is stated here so a future reader trading it away is trading a
// known quantity.
//
// WHAT `--required-regen-fixed-point` ACTUALLY MEASURES, CORRECTED (2026-08-19, fierce-ram-721
// review + operator verification, both against the source rather than the flag's name): despite
// its name, `run_required_regen_fixed_point` (required_regen_host.rs) never builds or invokes a
// candidate binary. `compile_stage0` calls `compile_sources`, a Rust fn linked into the SAME
// running claim_executor process that already emitted pass 1 -- there is no `Command::new` for
// cargo or a candidate executable anywhere in that path. So `fixed_point_equal` compares two
// emissions from the same in-process G0, not G0's emission against a rebuilt G1's. That is
// EMISSION REPEATABILITY (the compiler emits the same bytes twice), a real and useful property,
// but it is not a self-host fixed point, which requires G0 emit R1, BUILD G1 from R1, run G1 to
// emit R2, and compare R1 to R2 -- the build-and-run leg does not exist in this binary. The step
// below is named for what it measures, not for the CLI flag's name, to avoid publishing an
// authoritative CI green for a proposition nothing here evaluates (DESIGN §4b: a carrier named
// for a guarantee it does not hold is rung inflation, and ranks worse than sitting low, because
// it never gets fixed). The real self-host fixed point -- G0 builds and invokes G1 -- remains
// unanswered; a modeled v2 route (src/v2/compiler/self_host.dag) exists but has zero production
// consumers and sits in gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze, so it cannot hold that boundary today
// either. Whoever builds the real G0->G1->R2 chain should prove it with a candidate-use canary:
// swap the built candidate for a binary that prints a marker and exits nonzero, and confirm the
// boundary observes it -- a mechanism that only recomputes through G0 stays green and is caught.
// WHY THESE TWO STEPS DECLARE A PRECONDITION INSTEAD OF INHERITING THEIR POSITION.
//
// A step with no `if` inherits GitHub's default `success()`, which means the conjunction of
// every earlier step in the job. Ordered after the witness floor, both regen steps therefore
// carried an undeclared dependency on the floor's verdict -- and the regen claim does not
// depend on the floor's verdict. Its actual precondition is that the binary exists.
//
// Two costs, and the second is the reason this is a defect rather than a preference. The
// measurement cost: on the only run that has ever carried these steps (32312549861), both
// report `skipped` with zero duration, because the floor went red for main's own corpus
// defect -- a population with no relation to regen. So the regen wall cannot be measured on
// CI at all while any unrelated floor red stands. The correctness cost: once merged, a floor
// red DISARMS the regen gate, so the stale-mirror class this enrollment exists to catch goes
// unchecked on exactly the runs where the tree is already known to be unhealthy -- and a
// skipped step does not report as failing, so the check surface reads `floor red, regen fine`
// when regen was never asked. That is the empty-observation narrow from DESIGN's failure-mode
// list -- same class, new instance: bottom-as-answer conflated with bottom-as-ignorance, here
// `regen did not fail` standing in for `regen was never evaluated`. The named instance in
// DESIGN is a diff observation rendered as `nothing is affected`; this one is a skipped gate
// rendered as an unbroken gate.
//
// Binding to the build step's own outcome states the real dependency: the two claims are
// independent, they fail independently, and neither silences the other. This is a narrowing
// of an undeclared condition to a declared one, NOT a climb -- nothing here checks that a
// future step keeps declaring its precondition rather than inheriting one.
fn witness_regen_precondition() -> String {
  concat(witness_step_status_guard(), "steps.build_witness_fold.outcome == 'success'")
}

// The fixed-point step's precondition is the REGEN step, not the build, and this is measured
// rather than assumed: run standalone on a clean tree it exits nonzero in under a second with
// `refused: read receipt target/stage0-regen-receipt.json: No such file or directory`. It
// consumes a receipt the regen step above produces, so that is a genuine data dependency and
// the two steps are NOT peers. Binding it to the build alone would have replaced an undeclared
// condition that was too strong with one that is too weak -- it would run on every build and
// refuse for a missing receipt, reporting a receipt-plumbing failure where the real event is
// that regen did not get far enough to write one.
fn witness_regen_fixed_point_precondition() -> String {
  concat(witness_step_status_guard(), "steps.required_regen.outcome == 'success'")
}

fn witness_required_regen_script() -> String {
  concat(
    concat("ROOT=", concat(ci_repo_root_shell(), "\n")),
    concat(
      "\"$ROOT/target/release/claim_executor\" --required-regen",
      concat(witness_floor_source_root_flags(), "\n")
    )
  )
}

fn witness_required_regen_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Regen fixed point: first generation matches committed candidate" },
    id: Present { value: "required_regen" },
    run: witness_required_regen_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_regen_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn witness_required_regen_fixed_point_script() -> String {
  concat(
    concat("ROOT=", concat(ci_repo_root_shell(), "\n")),
    concat(
      "\"$ROOT/target/release/claim_executor\" --required-regen-fixed-point",
      concat(witness_floor_source_root_flags(), "\n")
    )
  )
}

fn witness_required_regen_fixed_point_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Regen determinism: G0 emit reproduces itself on a second pass" },
    id: none,
    run: witness_required_regen_fixed_point_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_regen_fixed_point_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// A DECLARED PROVISIONAL BOUND, NOT A DERIVED ONE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE POINT.
//
// Absent, this job inherits GitHub's 360-minute default -- so it was never unbounded, but
// the bound was nobody's decision and it is longer than any observed run needs. The cost it
// imposes is not wall clock: a self-hosted runner is held for the duration, and the step's
// log is UNREADABLE until the job ends, so an over-long ceiling converts a failed run into
// hours of zero information. A bounded failure is strictly more informative than an
// unbounded wait.
//
// 180 is NOT derived from a completed run, because no run of this fold has ever completed.
// Deriving it would require the completion time this bound exists to force. What it is: half
// the inherited default, above every phase sequence measured so far (preparation 553s plus
// published-mock projection 47s locally, at bfecfd961c1), and low enough that the log
// arrives the same working hour as the failure. Calling it derived would be the fabricated
// plausible output DESIGN section 5 forbids, one level up -- a number with no measurement
// behind it wearing a measurement's authority.
//
// The two regen steps added 2026-08-19 are covered by the SAME job-level bound, provisionally,
// for the same reason: neither has ever completed in CI either, so there is nothing to derive a
// separate figure from yet. This is a widening of what the existing provisional number covers,
// not a second number -- DESIGN §2 (one concept, one authority) applies to a timeout bound as
// much as to any other fact.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: the first run that completes this fold, floor and regen alike. Real wall
// times replace this constant -- at which point this note dissolves with the provisional bound
// it explains.
fn witness_floor_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: witness_floor_workflow_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: [
      witness_floor_checkout_step(),
      witness_floor_toolchain_step(),
      witness_floor_build_step(),
      witness_floor_v1_parse_step(),
      witness_required_regen_step(),
      witness_required_regen_fixed_point_step(),
      witness_floor_run_step()
    ],
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: none,
    timeout_minutes: 180,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

data witness_floor_workflow: Workflow = {
  name: witness_floor_workflow_name,
  on: [
    WorkflowDispatch { inputs: [] },
    Push { branches: [gunbc_default_branch_name], paths: [] },
    PullRequest {
      branches: [gunbc_default_branch_name],
      types: [Opened, Synchronize, Reopened, ReadyForReview]
    }
  ],
  concurrency: Present {
    value: ConcurrencyMappingQueueNotMax {
      group: witness_floor_concurrency_group,
      cancel_in_progress: Present {
        value: CancelInProgressExpression {
          expression: "${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}"
        }
      },
      explicit_single: none
    }
  },
  env: Present {
    value: [
      kv(key: "CARGO_TERM_COLOR", value: yaml_string(s: "always")),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_EXPECTED_RED_ROSTER_JOIN", value: yaml_string(s: "expected_red_roster_join.tsv"))
    ]
  },
  permissions: Present {
    value: WorkflowPermissions {
      contents: Present { value: PermRead },
      pull_requests: none,
      issues: none,
      actions: none,
      id_token: none
    }
  },
  jobs: [witness_floor_job()]
}

fn expected_witness_floor_yml() -> String {
  serialize_yaml(v: project_workflow_to_yaml(workflow: witness_floor_workflow))
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FIRST-EXECUTION FINDINGS (2026-08-15). Three rulings, recorded here beside the emission they
// are about rather than in a plan document that would drift from it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

data witness_floor_first_execution_receipt: String = "THE FOLD'S FIRST REAL EXECUTION REFUSED, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. Run on GitHub (12m24s whole job) and reproduced locally (531s for the fold alone): strict preparation refused with one typed located diagnostic and ZERO witnesses executed. Measured cost shape inside the fold — frontend 38s, normalize 2s, reconcile ~8 minutes, analyses 1s; reconcile IS the fold. Diagnostic census after repairing this cut's own residue: 282 diagnostics across 75 files (57 test/fixture, 18 production). CORRECTION 2026-08-15: this sentence first read that ZERO of them referenced anything this cut deleted. That was FALSE, and the method that produced it is the error worth keeping. The check was a grep of the refusal set for `ci_floor|falsifier|selection|scoped_witness|pr_native|fast_lane` — a list of names I expected — so it could only ever find residue I had already thought of. Two carriers declared in the deleted `affected_set_floor_runner` (`FloorDiffLineTouch`, `DiffPathsProduced`) matched none of those patterns and sat in the census unattributed, along with the two witness files that consumed them. The sound check, run afterwards, asks the question from the other side: for every unresolved symbol in the refusal set, is it declared anywhere in the live tree, and if not, was it declared in a file this branch deleted. That found them immediately. A grep for names you predict is not a census; it is a confirmation of your own list, and this is the second time in one day this lane produced an incomplete enumeration while documenting the incomplete-enumeration class. Against the old floor's 56.5 minutes this is 8m51s, but the comparison is NOT like-for-like and must not be quoted as one: a refusing run that executed nothing is cheaper than a run that executed 9,000 witnesses for reasons that have nothing to do with the fold's design."

data witness_floor_whole_tree_strict_concealment_note: String = "WHAT THE REFUSAL ACTUALLY DISCOVERED: this repository has never been whole-tree strict-typechecked. The old floor prepared PER-ENTRY closures, so a broken module reddened only the closures that imported it and was invisible everywhere else; a module nothing imported was never typechecked at all. This fold's admission wall is the first mechanism that has ever demanded the whole tree resolve as one namespace, and it cannot yet. That is a pre-existing concealment surfaced, not a regression introduced — and it is a larger finding than the fold's wall-clock. Note the same asymmetry inside this lane's own verification: `gunbc compile --target dag` resolves a transitive import closure and reports the remaining 1,135 modules as census-only, which is why this cut's own six orphaned importers passed a whole-corpus compile and were caught only by the fold."

data witness_floor_all_or_nothing_upheld_note: String = "ALL-OR-NOTHING PREPARATION IS UPHELD (ruling 2026-08-15), and the reasoning is recorded because the temptation to relax it recurs. The subject IS the corpus. Admitting a partially-typechecking subject while reporting a whole-corpus verdict is the empty-observation narrow — rendering 'I could not admit this population' as 'this population is fine' — which DESIGN names as strictly worse than the widen section 5 already forbids, because a widen is merely expensive while a narrow is silently uncovered. The ordering consequence is real and is owned by the operator rather than by this lane: the fold's first green day requires the last pre-existing defect to be fixed."

data witness_floor_bare_cross_module_reference_class_note: String = "THE LARGEST DIAGNOSTIC CLASS IS NOT THIS LANE'S TO FIX, and it must not be fixed the obvious way. 151 of the 185 undefined-variable diagnostics are one name: `Empty`. CORRECTED 2026-08-15, same day, against this note's own first revision: that revision said `Empty` is the nullary constructor of `FreeMonoid` in `std.algebra`, declared once. It is declared TWICE — `std.stack` `Stack` and `std.algebra` `FreeMonoid` — and the error was reproduced independently on both sides of the relay, because each of us grepped the module we had been pointed at and neither swept the tree for a second declaration of the name. That is the incomplete-enumeration class committed while diagnosing the incomplete-enumeration class, and it is recorded here rather than quietly amended because the corrected fact changes the remedy.\n\nWHAT THESE SITES ACTUALLY ARE: bare references to a TWO-CANDIDATE HOMONYM, where neither candidate sits on the referencing module's containment chain (verified on the settling specimen, `v2.lens.enforcement.cost_coverage`, which carries exactly one unrelated import and uses bare `Empty` in its body). The old per-entry path admitted them by POOL-MEMBERSHIP COINCIDENCE — some unrelated entry in that closure happened to drag the right module into the pool — which is the corpus-wide accidental-coverage trap DESIGN already carries. Strict whole-corpus preparation has no such coincidence to rely on.\n\nTHE REMEDY, and what it is NOT: containment-based resolution cannot bind these, because there is nothing on the chain to bind them to; the honest answer at those sites is a refusal for ambiguity. So this is corpus AUTHORING work — each site takes a qualified reference (`std.algebra.Empty`) — and NOT resolver work. The first revision of this note recorded the fold's first green as downstream of the namespace cut's resolution model; that dependency is STRUCK, because it was a promise made on the namespace lane's behalf that its own measurement showed it could not keep. DO NOT REPAIR BY ADDING IMPORT LINES: that ruling stands unchanged, and its reason is now sharper — a qualified reference IS the dependency edge, which is the end state; an import line is the grammar being deleted.\n\nNOT STARTED HERE, deliberately: 151 qualified references authored on this branch would collide with the namespace branch's rewrite of the same files, so sequencing across two live branches is an operator decision. The single dual-std `nat_compare` ambiguity (`std.nat` vs `v2.std.nat`) is the same shape and confirms the pattern is the dual std tree under one flat namespace rather than an `Empty` quirk."

data witness_floor_memory_envelope_finding: String = "THE FOLD'S MEMORY ENVELOPE IS A FIRST-CLASS PROPERTY, measured rather than estimated. One run was OOM-KILLED (exit 137 at 537s, during strict preparation — a kill, not a refusal). The next run was sampled: RSS climbed 3.9 -> 4.8 -> 5.8 -> 6.3 GiB over 90 seconds and PEAKED AT 9.37 GiB. The container cap (31 GiB) is not the binding constraint; the shared 20 GiB slice is, and the kernel kills the LARGEST task in it when the aggregate is exceeded, which is the likely cause of the kill. CONSEQUENCE, named because it is what makes this rank beside the diagnostic census: non-deterministic OOM on a shared host is exactly the shape that makes a required check flaky, and a check that dies for reasons unrelated to its subject teaches everyone to ignore it. DIRECTION RULED, MEASUREMENT NOT STARTED: do NOT re-shard into batches or worker processes to make it fit — that is the deleted decomposition rebuilding itself inside the replacement one convenience at a time, and the single prepared subject is precisely what the fold exists to be. The 9.37 GiB peak is a cost-shape defect to root-cause (the repository already carries that lane: schedule-derived per-module retention, and the carrier/interning roots), not a reason to change the shape."
